      "solution": "<p><em>A: Prior to Python 3.11, entering a <code>try</code> block executed <code>SETUP_FINALLY</code> bytecode instructions that pushed an exception handler onto the block stack. In Python 3.11 (PEP 657 / zero-cost exception handling), the compiler emits zero setup instructions on the happy path; instead, it compiles static table mappings of bytecode ranges to handler offsets. If no exception occurs, the <code>try</code> block executes with zero runtime overhead. However, when an exception is actually raised, the runtime pays the cost of allocating the exception instance, creating traceback objects, and materializing frame objects. Hence, exceptions are free on the happy path, but slow on the error path, making them inappropriate for standard loop termination in tight compute loops.</em></p>",
